 ## Overview
-Depth-Supervised NeRF (DS-NeRF) is a training procedure that accelerates Neural Radiance Field optimization and improves quality in sparse-view settings by incorporating depth supervision from sparse 3D keypoints generated during Structure-from-Motion (SfM) preprocessing [S1]. Unlike methods relying on data-driven priors from other scenes or semantic consistency, DS-NeRF imposes a loss that forces the rendered ray termination distribution to match the surface priors provided by these keypoints, effectively enforcing that geometry consists of empty space and opaque surfaces [S1]. This approach is complementary to standard NeRF and can be combined with other optimization procedures to reduce overfitting and speed up training by 2-3x [S1].
+Depth-Supervised NeRF (DS-NeRF) is a training procedure that accelerates Neural Radiance Field optimization and improves quality in sparse-view settings by incorporating depth supervision from sparse 3D keypoints generated during Structure-from-Motion (SfM) preprocessing [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on data-driven priors from other scenes or semantic consistency, DS-NeRF imposes a loss that forces the rendered ray termination distribution to match the surface priors given by these keypoints, effectively regularizing the density field $\sigma$ without requiring additional training data or domain-specific assumptions [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Inputs:** Input images $I_j$, camera poses $P_j$ (estimated via SfM), sparse 3D keypoints $X$ (estimated via SfM), and near/far clipping bounds $t_n, t_f$.
-*   **Outputs:** Optimized NeRF model parameters (density $\sigma$ and color $c$).
+*   **Inputs:** A set of $N$ input images $\{I_j\}$, their camera poses $\{P_j\}$ (estimated via SfM), and the resulting sparse 3D keypoints $\{X\}$ with visibility flags [S1].
+*   **Outputs:** A trained NeRF model (MLPs for density $\sigma$ and color $c$) capable of rendering novel views [S1].
 
 **Data Structures**
-*   **Keypoints:** A set $X = \{x_1, x_2, \dots\} \subset \mathbb{R}^3$.
-*   **Visibility:** For each camera $j$, a subset $X_j \subset X$ of keypoints visible from that camera.
-*   **Depth Estimates:** For each visible keypoint $x_i \in X_j$, a scalar depth $D_{ij}$ and uncertainty $\hat{\sigma}_i$.
+*   **Keypoints:** A set $X = \{x_1, x_2, \dots\} \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ representing 3D points reconstructed by SfM [S1].
+*   **Visibility:** For each camera $j$, a subset $X_j \subset X$ of keypoints visible from that camera [S1].
+*   **Depth Estimates:** For each visible keypoint $x_i \in X_j$, a scalar depth $D_{ij}$ and an uncertainty variance $\hat{\sigma}_i^2$ [S1].
 
